{
  "file": "crates/trust-but-verify/src/agent/tests.rs",
  "item": "impl[CachedProvider/SourceProvider]/fn[read]",
  "citations": [
    {
      "role": "cite",
      "name": "habeas:coverage",
      "commentary": "The provider overrides parse with a cached snapshot. Selected-source\n reads must deliver and fingerprint that snapshot without fetching again;\n this trait-required callback rejects a refetch. Primitive provider reads\n are covered separately, and executing this guard would fail this test."
    }
  ],
  "determination": true,
  "rationale": "The `habeas:coverage` citation on `crates/trust-but-verify/src/agent/tests.rs#impl[CachedProvider/SourceProvider]/fn[read]` is a valid coverage-exception proposal.\n\n- **Placement:** It sits on an explicit function with a body, which the built-in allows. Its explanation is in fenced citation commentary, so it counts as a tracked justification.\n- **Substance:** The reason is not just difficulty. `CachedProvider` overrides `parse` to return a cached snapshot through `parse_snapshot`. `read` exists only because the trait requires it. It returns `SourceError::Invalid(\"must not refetch\")`, which is a guard that fails the test if it runs. Executing it would therefore fail the test. That explains why it has zero execution.\n- **Agreement with the source:** The `impl[CachedProvider/SourceProvider]` block shows `read` as a stub error and `parse` as the cached-snapshot path. The sibling test `cached_selected_source_reads_record_exact_delivered_contents_and_errors_add_no_input` builds its `SourceAnalysis` on `CachedProvider` and reads through the parsed snapshot. This matches the claim about selected-source reads.\n- **Primitive reads:** The claim that primitive provider reads are covered elsewhere holds. `crates/trust-but-verify/src/sources/tests.rs` has providers whose `read` returns real results or errors: `Primitives`, `Memory` and `FailingProvider`.\n- **Other rules:** The citation does not also carry `{impl}` or `{test}` for the same target, so it is not redundant. Nothing in the item conflicts with the built-in's text.",
